NXR-191|Random stream versioning|Failure reproduction|Randomized tests shall identify the generator algorithm and version as well as the seed and stream identifier.|A seed alone cannot reproduce a test after the generator or stream partition changes.|A golden vector reproduces across host and target builds; changing the algorithm without changing version fails.|Publish fixed seed vectors including zero and maximal seeds and compare independent implementations.|NXR-072,NXR-073,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Fixed generator state per worker; no entropy dependency for replay.|include/rtos_trace.hpp,include/rtos_fault_record.hpp,docs/hardware-in-loop.rst|A seed alone cannot reproduce a test after the generator or stream partition changes.
NXR-192|Schedule choice recording|Failure reproduction|Replay reports shall distinguish deterministic workload inputs from nondeterministic interrupt and scheduling choices.|Seeded jobs are reproducible even when their legal thread interleavings are not.|Identical seeds with different timer delivery produce separate schedule identities; exact replay is claimed only with sufficient recorded choices.|Repeat workloads with shifted interrupt points and compare input and schedule logs separately.|NXR-072,NXR-073,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Fixed trace ring with explicit loss counters; loss disables exact replay claims.|include/rtos_trace.hpp,include/rtos_fault_record.hpp,docs/hardware-in-loop.rst|Seeded jobs are reproducible even when their legal thread interleavings are not.
NXR-193|Job oracle independence|Failure reproduction|Each randomized job family shall have a reference calculation that does not reuse the tested implementation path.|A checksum of the implementation's own answer is not an independent correctness oracle.|A deliberate error in arithmetic, queue ordering or payload generation yields a mismatch even with a valid transport CRC.|Use separately authored reference functions and mutation fixtures for each job family.|NXR-072,NXR-073,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Fixed input and result sizes; bounded outstanding jobs.|include/rtos_trace.hpp,include/rtos_fault_record.hpp,docs/hardware-in-loop.rst|A checksum of the implementation's own answer is not an independent correctness oracle.
NXR-194|Replay bundle completeness|Failure reproduction|A replay bundle shall include firmware, source revision, dirty diff, tool versions, configuration, seed and the terminal classification.|A commit ID cannot identify uncommitted source or substituted firmware bytes.|Removing any mandatory identity makes the bundle incomplete; a clean and dirty build remain distinguishable.|Validate bundle schema against intentionally missing fields and hash mismatches.|NXR-072,NXR-073,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Host storage quota declared per run; archive exhaustion cannot be reported as a pass.|include/rtos_trace.hpp,include/rtos_fault_record.hpp,docs/hardware-in-loop.rst|A commit ID cannot identify uncommitted source or substituted firmware bytes.
NXR-195|First-fault retention scope|Failure reproduction|Fault reports shall state whether their storage survives only the current boot or a qualified reset domain.|The current FirstFault helper explicitly does not provide reset or double-fault retention.|Ordinary RAM is never labeled reset-retained; a later fault cannot overwrite the first fault in the same capture epoch.|Trigger two distinct faults and then each supported reset kind; compare retained identity and validity markers.|NXR-072,NXR-073,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|One fixed first-fault record per capture epoch; no allocation in capture.|include/rtos_trace.hpp,include/rtos_fault_record.hpp,docs/hardware-in-loop.rst|The current FirstFault helper explicitly does not provide reset or double-fault retention.
NXR-196|Trace overflow semantics|Failure reproduction|Trace consumers shall report lost events and forbid complete-history conclusions after overflow.|A bounded trace is useful evidence only when omitted history remains explicit.|Fill the ring, drop events, drain and refill; loss count is visible and saturates without wrapping.|Exercise capacity one, full and counter-boundary fixtures and malformed consumer sequence claims.|NXR-072,NXR-073,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Configured event capacity only; overflow never blocks producers.|include/rtos_trace.hpp,include/rtos_fault_record.hpp,docs/hardware-in-loop.rst|A bounded trace is useful evidence only when omitted history remains explicit.
NXR-197|Failure minimization preservation|Failure reproduction|A failure reducer shall preserve the original bundle and emit every reduced candidate as a separately identified artifact.|Simplifying a randomized failure can accidentally remove the trigger while retaining a similar symptom.|A reduced workload that stops reproducing is rejected as a reproducer; original inputs remain immutable.|Replay a seeded injected failure, remove operations and verify stable failure identity.|NXR-072,NXR-073,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Finite reduction attempts and host time budget; no autonomous unbounded search.|include/rtos_trace.hpp,include/rtos_fault_record.hpp,docs/hardware-in-loop.rst|Simplifying a randomized failure can accidentally remove the trigger while retaining a similar symptom.
NXR-198|Mismatch first witness|Failure reproduction|A result mismatch shall retain the first worker, job sequence, operation and expected versus observed value before summarizing signatures.|Aggregate mismatch counts do not identify the operation needed to reproduce a defect.|Two mismatches preserve the earliest witness; report truncation cannot erase its identity.|Inject a known bad job followed by a second fault and saturate normal telemetry.|NXR-072,NXR-073,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|One fixed witness record plus bounded context window.|include/rtos_trace.hpp,include/rtos_fault_record.hpp,docs/hardware-in-loop.rst|Aggregate mismatch counts do not identify the operation needed to reproduce a defect.
NXR-199|Endurance completion criteria|Failure reproduction|An endurance result shall require its declared target operations and target duration independently of host cutoff.|A twelve-hour host window does not imply twelve hours of simulated target execution.|A host deadline reached before target goals reports wall limit or incomplete rather than passed.|Run deliberately slow simulation with short host cutoff and a separate completed control.|NXR-072,NXR-073,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Finite host cutoff plus cleanup allowance; target goals recorded before start.|include/rtos_trace.hpp,include/rtos_fault_record.hpp,docs/hardware-in-loop.rst|A twelve-hour host window does not imply twelve hours of simulated target execution.
NXR-200|Detection qualification campaign|Failure reproduction|Each claimed invariant checker shall detect at least one controlled violation of its own property.|Healthy runs alone cannot demonstrate that diagnostic checks discriminate failures.|Disabled or always-successful checkers fail qualification; every injected violation has an expected classification.|Create explicit controls for context corruption, duplicate jobs, stale handles and bad UART content.|NXR-072,NXR-073,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Fault injection confined to named test profiles; no release default activation.|include/rtos_trace.hpp,include/rtos_fault_record.hpp,docs/hardware-in-loop.rst|Healthy runs alone cannot demonstrate that diagnostic checks discriminate failures.
